summary:
I am not going to be drawn into the AI Doom conversation. @perrymetzger has been making many of the points I would; check out some of his threads if you want another perspective.
Quoting @perrymetzger
@AlexVeshev @tszzl As I’ve repeatedly noted, John von Neumann was convinced, based on what he thought was an airtight game theory argument, that a nuclear war was inevitable and a first strike against the Soviets was necessary.

It is counter intuitive, but forming Voltron out of all the possible pieces in play very often isn’t the best way to achieve a goal. Many strategies are incompatible, and nobody has line of sight on an AI endgame today, so we need to maintain some diversity of approach.
Quoting @aweknowing
@elonmusk @alx People need to team up when possible instead of wanting to do everything themselves @elonmusk please talks to @bengoertzel and @ID_AA_Carmack and @EMostaque about partnerships and collaboration

That raises an interesting question — why aren’t there WiFi/RF targeting drone destroyers? They are shining a beacon! No AI needed.
Quoting @DogeMarineP
@ID_AA_Carmack can we use AI to train a wifi system to detect and eliminate small antipersonnel drones with an automated autoshotty turret? Using the wifi as the detection field

A good example of looking deeper than the sound bite headline — most research papers are nowhere near as conclusive as many people think.
Quoting @NYarvin
@tlbtlbtlb This seemed rather high, so I read the paper. I don't believe it. The plastic they get the high numbers for was polyethylene, and the method that gave them that number was Py-GC/MS. That's pyrolysis fed into gas chromatography/mass spectrometry.

clean_summary:
I thought no way is this real, but there actually is a project along these lines. I doubt Nvidia is more involved than just selling GPUs, and they aren’t going to be silent, but a company is actually pitching this.

Large data centers have substantial efficiencies of scale, so this is economically nonsensical even for low-grade inference, unless traditional construction is outlawed.

@HarryRamstrong: "Nvidia is testing fridge-sized boxes to be parked at people's homes. The unit is silent, small, and unintrusive. Nvidia pays electric bills and provides a monthly stipend. That's how you build data centers!"
